WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.037 --> 00:00:10.080
Will sat at his desk, staring at a picture in his textbook.

2
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.283
"How can huge holes just appear underground?

3
00:00:13.803 --> 00:00:15.624
I don't understand," he thought.

4
00:00:17.686 --> 00:00:19.308
He noticed it was already ten o'clock.

5
00:00:19.328 --> 00:00:22.991
"I'm so tired I can't even think anymore!

6
00:00:23.551 --> 00:00:27.314
I'll finish this report tomorrow morning," he decided and climbed into bed.

7
00:00:27.815 --> 00:00:29.276
Within minutes he was asleep.

8
00:00:31.576 --> 00:00:32.636
Will woke up shivering.

9
00:00:33.076 --> 00:00:35.917
He opened his eyes but saw nothing—just blackness!

10
00:00:36.597 --> 00:00:39.438
It was eerily quiet, except for a constant dripping.

11
00:00:40.919 --> 00:00:44.400
Then QT's screen lit up, and Will realized he wasn't in his room anymore.

12
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.601
"You are in Mammoth Cave, in Kentucky," QT said.

13
00:00:49.621 --> 00:00:53.922
"This is the world's longest cave system.

14
00:00:54.443 --> 00:00:59.124
People have explored almost six hundred kilometers of tunnels and caverns so far!"

15
00:01:01.056 --> 00:01:03.838
"I wouldn't want to get lost down here," Will murmured.

16
00:01:04.298 --> 00:01:08.161
Then, trying to be brave, he asked, "How are caves formed, anyway?"

17
00:01:09.762 --> 00:01:11.944
"Well, there are many types of caves.

18
00:01:12.524 --> 00:01:16.767
I'll show you," QT said and projected an image of a cliff on the cave wall.

19
00:01:16.787 --> 00:01:24.353
"Sea caves are made by waves beating on rock for a very long time, hundreds and thousands of

20
00:01:24.413 --> 00:01:28.896
years. The wind can also carve out caves.

21
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.201
You often see these in deserts.

22
00:01:31.782 --> 00:01:33.783
There are caves in lava tubes too.

23
00:01:35.564 --> 00:01:41.868
When lava is flowing from a volcano, the top gets cool first and hardens, but the hot lava

24
00:01:41.948 --> 00:01:45.371
inside flows right out, leaving a cave behind.

25
00:01:46.932 --> 00:01:54.136
Sometimes caves are even formed in glaciers when water flows through the cracks, making them into big holes."

26
00:01:56.718 --> 00:01:59.300
Will looked at the fantastic formations on the ceiling.

27
00:01:59.724 --> 00:02:01.585
"This cave doesn't look like any of those."

28
00:02:01.605 --> 00:02:06.088
"We are in a solution cave, the most common type.

29
00:02:07.368 --> 00:02:12.731
This kind of cave is formed when water trickles through a layer of limestone in the ground to

30
00:02:12.771 --> 00:02:16.273
the water table, the level where our groundwater starts."

31
00:02:18.254 --> 00:02:19.215
Will was amazed.

32
00:02:19.795 --> 00:02:20.896
"Water made this cave?"

33
00:02:22.436 --> 00:02:24.137
"Well, it had a little help.

34
00:02:24.738 --> 00:02:28.860
While water is traveling through soil, it becomes slightly acidic.

35
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:37.524
Limestone dissolves easily in acid—sort of like sugar in hot tea— so when water flows through cracks in

36
00:02:37.564 --> 00:02:41.526
limestone for thousands of years, it forms these enormous caverns.

37
00:02:43.187 --> 00:02:48.250
When the water table drops, these caves are left empty, or rather, full of air."

38
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:53.472
"So that's what makes the holes, but what makes these weird rocks?"

39
00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:54.633
Will asked.

40
00:02:54.653 --> 00:02:56.594
"I'm getting to that.

41
00:02:57.214 --> 00:03:02.302
When the acidic water dissolves limestone, it absorbs the mineral from the stone.

42
00:03:03.623 --> 00:03:09.828
When the water drips from the ceiling of a cavern, it encounters air again and the opposite happens;

43
00:03:10.508 --> 00:03:12.750
the mineral dissolved in the water comes out.

44
00:03:14.612 --> 00:03:19.816
Every time a water droplet falls, a little of that mineral stays on the ceiling.

45
00:03:20.937 --> 00:03:27.222
Over time the mineral builds up to form stalactites, these cones hanging from the ceiling.

46
00:03:28.363 --> 00:03:35.177
When the drop hits the floor, a little of the mineral is deposited there too, which builds stalagmites,

47
00:03:35.538 --> 00:03:37.238
the cones rising from the floor."

48
00:03:37.258 --> 00:03:43.840
"Stalactites and stalagmites I'll never remember which is which!"

49
00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:45.180
Will interrupted.

50
00:03:46.161 --> 00:03:54.263
"Here's a trick to help you keep them straight: stalactites hold tight to the ceiling, and stalagmites might

51
00:03:54.443 --> 00:03:56.603
reach the ceiling," QT offered.

52
00:03:56.623 --> 00:04:02.397
"Anyway, they sometimes grow so long that they meet in the middle, forming a column."

53
00:04:02.417 --> 00:04:05.458
"Cool," Will remarked.

54
00:04:05.958 --> 00:04:09.179
As he continued down the tunnel, he saw another huge formation.

55
00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:10.219
"What's that?"

56
00:04:10.239 --> 00:04:12.780
"It's flowstone.

57
00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:16.561
It's really common in caves," QT explained.

58
00:04:16.581 --> 00:04:22.643
"And those thin tubes hanging from the ceiling are called soda straws, for obvious reasons."

59
00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:25.764
Will reached for one, and QT beeped in alarm.

60
00:04:26.188 --> 00:04:27.749
"Don't touch the formations!

61
00:04:27.909 --> 00:04:29.109
They're very fragile!"

62
00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.051
After Will pulled his hand back, QT continued.

63
00:04:33.071 --> 00:04:37.733
"They can break, and the oil on our hands interferes with their growth."

64
00:04:37.753 --> 00:04:39.894
"Oh. Okay."

65
00:04:40.374 --> 00:04:42.915
Will didn't try to touch anything else as he walked along.

66
00:04:43.815 --> 00:04:48.858
Finally QT said, "Well, we've reached the deepest part of this cave system.

67
00:04:49.738 --> 00:04:51.279
It's also the youngest part.

68
00:04:51.779 --> 00:04:54.380
We're over one hundred meters below the surface.

69
00:04:55.572 --> 00:04:58.113
You know you've reached the bottom when you see lots of water.

70
00:04:58.593 --> 00:05:01.054
There are many underground rivers and lakes."

71
00:05:01.074 --> 00:05:03.994
"And there's something swimming in the water!"

72
00:05:04.474 --> 00:05:05.195
Will exclaimed.

73
00:05:05.215 --> 00:05:11.336
"Believe it or not, certain species spend their entire lives in caves.

74
00:05:11.896 --> 00:05:15.798
Caves are unique because the conditions inside them stay the same all year.

75
00:05:17.278 --> 00:05:20.719
It's always dark, humid, and the temperature doesn't change much.

76
00:05:22.179 --> 00:05:24.700
These species have adapted to help them survive here.

77
00:05:26.012 --> 00:05:32.656
Like many other cave creatures, that species of fish has lived in complete darkness so long that it's

78
00:05:32.676 --> 00:05:33.516
gone blind.

79
00:05:34.076 --> 00:05:35.977
But it's very sensitive to movement."

80
00:05:35.997 --> 00:05:38.279
"It's completely white too!"

81
00:05:38.299 --> 00:05:41.941
"Most cave species are very pale or white.

82
00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:48.164
Since no animal can see without light, there's no need to spend valuable energy developing color."

83
00:05:49.625 --> 00:05:52.346
Will walked up a steep tunnel branching away from the river.

84
00:05:52.866 --> 00:05:55.488
After a few minutes, he leaned against the wall to rest.

85
00:05:57.172 --> 00:05:59.493
"Ahhh!" Will screamed and jumped away.

86
00:05:59.513 --> 00:06:01.273
"Something touched my hand!"

87
00:06:02.293 --> 00:06:04.054
He looked closely and saw a little bug.

88
00:06:04.074 --> 00:06:08.715
"That's just a cave cricket," QT explained.

89
00:06:08.735 --> 00:06:10.596
"It's harmless.

90
00:06:11.056 --> 00:06:13.837
Its long limbs and antennae are another adaptation.

91
00:06:14.337 --> 00:06:18.118
They help the cricket find food and avoid predators, since it can't see."

92
00:06:19.218 --> 00:06:22.619
Will continued to climb, and soon a glimmer of light appeared ahead.

93
00:06:23.079 --> 00:06:26.080
He reached the top of the tunnel and crawled inside a huge cave.

94
00:06:27.432 --> 00:06:31.675
"Yikes!" Will froze when he spotted dozens of bats flying near the ceiling.

95
00:06:31.695 --> 00:06:34.997
"Don't be afraid, Will," QT said.

96
00:06:35.017 --> 00:06:36.558
"Bats aren't dangerous.

97
00:06:37.099 --> 00:06:41.722
They're quite useful; a single bat can eat three thousand bugs a night!

98
00:06:42.763 --> 00:06:49.967
Because they hunt in darkness—in caves and outside at night—they rely on sound instead of sight to find

99
00:06:50.047 --> 00:06:57.495
insects." A bat flew so close to Will's face that he felt the breeze from its wings.

100
00:06:58.075 --> 00:07:00.877
He closed his eyes and took a deep breath before moving again.

101
00:07:02.519 --> 00:07:05.581
When he opened his eyes, he saw that he was back in his room.

102
00:07:06.121 --> 00:07:07.762
A breeze drifted in through the window.

103
00:07:07.782 --> 00:07:14.307
"Whew. I'm glad I don't live in a cave," he said, "but maybe I'll ask Mom if we

104
00:07:14.327 --> 00:07:15.028
can visit one."
